Reject out-of-order extents when reading a bmbt (M2 final review I2).

Why: xfs_iread_bmbt_block() stuffs every record of every leaf into the incore
extent map. The only sibling check in xfs_btree_visit_blocks() is
`sibling == self`, so a two-block cycle A -> B -> A of valid bmbt leaves is
walked forever; the loop ends only when the number of loaded records exceeds
di_nextents, and di_nextents is bounded only by di_nblocks (which a crafted
inode can set to 2^32 or more). Each lap allocates 16 bytes per record in the
iext tree (with __GFP_NOFAIL), i.e. unbounded memory growth.
Why not a shim: the loop is inside libxfs; the shim sees neither the records
nor the sibling pointers.
Upstream: candidate (the same unbounded walk exists in the kernel; there the
extent count is checked, but a cycle with a huge di_nextents is not).
Behaviour: a record that starts before the end of the previous record
(valid trees are strictly ordered and non-overlapping) fails the read with
-EFSCORRUPTED, so a cycle dies on the second lap at the latest. Legitimate
trees are unaffected.

--- a/xfs_bmap.c
+++ b/xfs_bmap.c
@@ -1086,6 +1086,7 @@
 struct xfs_iread_state {
 	struct xfs_iext_cursor	icur;
 	xfs_extnum_t		loaded;
+	xfs_fileoff_t		next_off;	/* end of the previous record */
 };
 
 int
@@ -1163,6 +1164,20 @@
 			return xfs_bmap_complain_bad_rec(ip, whichfork, fa,
 					&new);
 		}
+		/*
+		 * Records are strictly ordered and never overlap in a valid
+		 * tree. A sibling cycle (A -> B -> A) passes every other check
+		 * and would otherwise be read again and again, growing the
+		 * incore extent map without bound.
+		 */
+		if (unlikely(new.br_startoff < ir->next_off)) {
+			xfs_inode_verifier_error(ip, -EFSCORRUPTED,
+					"xfs_iread_extents(3)", frp,
+					sizeof(*frp), __this_address);
+			xfs_bmap_mark_sick(ip, whichfork);
+			return -EFSCORRUPTED;
+		}
+		ir->next_off = new.br_startoff + new.br_blockcount;
 		xfs_iext_insert(ip, &ir->icur, &new,
 				xfs_bmap_fork_to_state(whichfork));
 		trace_xfs_read_extent(ip, &ir->icur,
@@ -1194,6 +1209,7 @@
 	xfs_assert_ilocked(ip, XFS_ILOCK_EXCL);
 
 	ir.loaded = 0;
+	ir.next_off = 0;
 	xfs_iext_first(ifp, &ir.icur);
 	cur = xfs_bmbt_init_cursor(mp, tp, ip, whichfork);
